    "answer": {
      "short": "Use change of base: $\\log_2(5)=\\dfrac{\\ln(5)}{\\ln(2)}$ or $\\dfrac{\\log_{10}(5)}{\\log_{10}(2)}$. He could have taken natural or common logarithms of both sides from the beginning.",
      "worked": "Bob must use the change-of-base formula:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\log_2(5)\\\\x&=\\frac{\\ln(5)}{\\ln(2)}\\\\x&\\approx2.32.\\end{align*}$$\nHe could instead have taken natural logarithms of both sides at the beginning:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\ln(2^x)&=\\ln(5)\\\\x\\ln(2)&=\\ln(5)\\\\x&=\\frac{\\ln(5)}{\\ln(2)}.\\end{align*}$$\nCommon logarithms would give the same result.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
